#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# skewness

#% text
# `skewness(d)` gives the skewness `E[(X - mu)^3]/sigma^3` of a distribution; `skewness(xs)` gives `m3/m2^(3/2)` of a data list.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
skewness(exponential_distribution(l))
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
skewness(poisson_distribution(m))
#% output
#= m^(-1/2)

#% cell
skewness([1, 2, 2, 3, 10])
#% output
#= (1476/17689)*266^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A symmetric distribution has no skewness:

#% cell
skewness(normal_distribution(mu, s))
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `kurtosis`, `variance`, `moment`, `mean`
